
\section{Multiply Accumulate (MAC) Design}

Two versions of the MAC were implemented for comparison of un-pipeline and piplined of the design. Both the unpipeline, 'mac' and pipelined, 'mac\_pipe' used used an accumulator register that requires one clock cycle at the rising-edge for the output to update for any changes in the input. The only difference between 'mac' and 'mac\_pipe' is the combinational logic of the multiply-add operation of the 'mac' that is separated into two stages in the 'mac\_pipe'. This mean, the multiplier output and associated control signals are stored in with an additional intermediate register before being added and sent to the accumulator in the next cycle. Thus, the pipelined version will require two cycles for the output to be observed compared to one cycle for the accumulator in the un-pipelined version. However, we will come to realize the benefits of pipelining when considering the efficiency of the whole system. 

