The Dynamic Power used in Figure {} is define as the sum of "Total Dynamic Power" and "Cell Leakage Power", where
"Total Dynmaic power" is jsut the sum of "Cell Internal Power" and "Net Switching Power" in the synthesis report. 
The cell leakage power can almost be neligible compare to the Dynamic power, this could be the case of the small
area of the design and that the accumulator register is the only memory component for a sigificant leakage power. 
Given a fixed accumulator width and a constant area at a frequency lower than 166.67 MHz, we can observed the 
leakage power also remain constant in assoication with the constant area in the table. Thus we can conclude that
leakage power depend on the area of the design, where the affects of frequency is the result of the slight increase
in area that correspond to an relative increase in leakage power. 

Thus the trend observed in figure {} of total power is largely contributed by the Dynamic Power in relationship with
increase in frequency. Therefore, increase in frequency has a linear effect on the amount power required for nets 
switching and cell internal. In order words, a faster design can perform more operation to consume more
power compare to a slower design that perform less operation in the same period of time. For this design, the amount
of power per single clock cycle counsume about 1.8 * 10^-6 uW of power, where power scales one-to-one with frequency. 




